---
title: 'Overlays'
---

There are some options to display custom HTML over a chart.

## Missing Data Overlay

Sometimes end-users can be confused if a chart doesn't have any content. To help them understand that no data has been supplied, a message is displayed over the chart area.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Overlay for Missing Data" name="no-data-plain" type="generated" /%}

## No Visible Series Overlay

A message is also displayed when all series are hidden:

{% chartExampleRunner title="No Visible Series" name="no-visible-series" type="generated" /%}

## Loading Data Overlay

When using [Asynchronous Data](./async-data/), a loading data animation is shown while waiting for a response.
The overlay can also be shown or hidden manually using the `loading` option. See [Asynchronous Data — Manual Control](./async-data/#manual-control) for details.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Loading Data" name="loading" type="generated" /%}

## Unsupported Browser Overlay

When an [unsupported browser](./supported-browsers/) is detected and the chart may not operate correctly, we display an overlay to make the user aware of this.

## Customisation

### Text

These messages can be customised through `overlays`:

```js format="snippet"
{
    overlays: {
        loading: {
            text: 'Some custom loading message',
        },
        noData: {
            text: 'Some custom noData message',
        },
        noVisibleSeries: {
            text: 'Some custom noVisibleSeries message',
        },
        unsupportedBrowser: {
            text: 'Some custom unsupportedBrowser message',
        },
    },
}
```

### Custom Overlay

If finer grained control is required, a renderer can be provided to allow full customisation:

{% chartExampleRunner title="Custom Overlay for Missing Data" name="no-data" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    overlays: {
        noData: {
            renderer: () => '<em>Custom message for <strong>missing data</strong></em>',
        },
    },
}
```

### Custom Loading Spinner

{% chartExampleRunner title="Custom Overlay for Loading Data" name="loading-custom" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    overlays: {
        loading: {
            renderer: () => {
                const container = document.createElement('div');
                // ... styles

                const spinner = document.createElement('div');
                // ... styles

                const animation = document.createElement('style');
                // ... keyframes

                container.append(spinner, animation);

                return container;
            },
        },
    },
}
```

## Validation Overlay

Option misconfiguration and caught runtime errors are normally reported only to the browser console, where they are easy to miss. The validation overlay surfaces them on the chart instead. Issues are grouped by severity — errors, then warnings, then deprecations — each group with its own count and accent colour, under a header summarising the totals.

The overlay is opt-in and intended for development. Enable it with `validations.overlayLevel`, which defaults to `'none'`:

{% chartExampleRunner title="Validation Overlay" name="validation-overlay" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    validations: {
        overlayLevel: 'warning',
    },
}
```

`overlayLevel` is an inclusive severity threshold: each level also shows every louder one.

- `'error'` — errors only.
- `'warning'` — errors and warnings.
- `'deprecation'` — errors, warnings and deprecations.
- `'none'` — nothing. This is the default, so the overlay never appears unless you opt in.

Each issue shows a message, a code block, and a Copy button for pasting into a bug report. Dismiss hides the overlay for that chart instance without suppressing future issues.

While shown, the validation overlay takes priority over the loading and no-data overlays.

